  "assumptions": [
    "Synthetic births are drawn from this list weighted by metro population, NOT from a uniform lat/lng box.",
    "Uniform global lat/lng would put roughly a quarter of sampled births in the Southern Ocean, and ascendant and house distributions are latitude-dependent, so the resulting fire rates would not describe any real population.",
    "Weights are approximate metro-area populations in millions, rounded. They are a proxy for birth density, not a measurement of it: current urban population over-represents cities that grew recently and under-represents rural births entirely.",
    "This means a published fire rate should be read as 'among charts distributed like today's world urban population', which is the assumption the product is willing to defend, not as 'among all humans ever born'.",
    "tz is the standard (non-DST) UTC offset in hours, used to convert a uniformly sampled LOCAL birth time into UTC. Because a uniform local time maps to a uniform UTC time, this does not change the distribution today; it is here so a future non-uniform birth-hour model stays correct.",
    "Coordinates are city-centre to two decimal places, which is well inside the precision that matters for a whole-sign house system."
  ],
